**Tianhai Company v. Yuedong Company — dispute over ship charter infringement**

**Plaintiff:** Tianjin International Shipping Company. Domicile: Yueyang Dao, Heping District, Tianjin.

**Legal Representative:** Song Xingting, Chairman of the Board of the company.

**Defendant:** Shantou Yuedong International Shipping Agency Co., Ltd. Domicile: Xidi Lu, Shantou City, Guangdong Province.

**Legal Representative:** Cai Kangmian, General Manager of the company.

The plaintiff, Tianjin International Shipping Company (hereinafter referred to as Tianhai Company), filed a lawsuit with the Tianjin Maritime Court against the defendant, Shantou Yuedong International Shipping Agency Co., Ltd. (hereinafter referred to as Yuedong Company), concerning a dispute over ship charter infringement.

**Plaintiff's Allegations:** The plaintiff had the right to operate and manage the M/V "SALVIA II". The defendant chartered the vessel to transport logs to Indonesia. As the cargo carried was suspected of being smuggled, the vessel was detained by Indonesian customs. During the detention, the defendant failed to come forward to explain the situation or assume responsibility, resulting in the vessel's seizure and causing the plaintiff to incur additional expenses and lose anticipated profits. The plaintiff seeks an order for the defendant to compensate the plaintiff for all losses totaling RMB 2,485,147.20 (all monetary units in this document are in RMB unless otherwise indicated) and to pay corresponding interest.

The plaintiff, Tianhai Company, submitted the following evidence:

1. A full-power authorization letter dated March 1, 1995, issued by Oriental Pioneer Shipping Co., Ltd. (hereinafter referred to as Pioneer Company) to Tianhai Company for the operation of the vessel;
2. A crew employment agreement and an extension agreement signed on August 1, 1999, and August 1, 2000, respectively, between Tianhai Company and the Shipping Department of China Guangdong International Cooperation (Group) Corporation;
3. A time charter party for the M/V "SALVIA II" dated April 17, 2000, signed between Tianhai Company, on behalf of Pioneer Company, and Ruihang International Co., Ltd. (hereinafter referred to as Ruihang Company);
4. A voyage charter party for the M/V "SALVIA II" dated April 25, 2000, signed between Ruihang Company and Yuedong Company;
5. The deck log of the M/V "SALVIA II" from May 5 to May 19, 2000;
6. Orders issued by Indonesian Customs in Sorong on May 19, 2000, requiring the M/V "SALVIA II" to proceed to the port and not to sail without approval, and an order dated May 20, 2000, to inspect the deck log and other items;
7. A certificate by Lin Huahui, master of the M/V "SALVIA II", regarding the joint inspection on May 9, 2000, the boarding of customs and immigration officials on May 16, 2000, and the detention of the vessel by Indonesian customs on July 14, 2000;
8. A memorandum prepared by Lin Huahui, master of the M/V "SALVIA II";
9. A receipt for relevant certificates taken from the M/V "SALVIA II" by the agent;
10. 67 pieces of correspondence (faxes, telexes, telegrams) between Tianhai Company, Ruihang Company, the M/V "SALVIA II", and Yuedong Company;
11. A report dated June 16, 2000, from Tianhai Company to the Tianjin Foreign Affairs Office regarding the detention of the M/V "SALVIA II" by Indonesian authorities;
12. Notes verbales addressed by the Chinese Embassy in Indonesia to the Indonesian Ministry of Foreign Affairs, the Attorney General's Office, and the Supreme Prosecutor's Office on June 26, July 6, and July 31, 2000, respectively;
13. A statement dated August 20, 2001, by Zhou Xikang, former First Secretary of the Chinese Embassy in Indonesia, regarding the situation of the M/V "SALVIA II";
14. The criminal judgment of the Sorong District Court in Indonesia against Lin Huahui, master of the M/V "SALVIA II";
15. A statement dated May 20, 2001, issued by Ye Sunfa, an Indonesian Chinese, in Sorong, Indonesia, regarding the situation of the M/V "SALVIA II";
16. In connection with the seizure of the M/V "SALVIA II": 5 penalty receipts, 5 lawyer fee receipts, 6 agency fee receipts, 5 translation fee receipts, 34 entertainment expense receipts for expenses paid to Ye Sunfa (Indonesian Chinese), 1 reward fee receipt for relevant personnel, and 29 travel expense receipts and 11 transportation/communication fee receipts incurred by Tianhai Company in handling the vessel seizure;
17. Vessel registration certificate, proving that the owner of the M/V "SALVIA II" is Pioneer Company;
18. Panama registration certificate, proving the establishment of Pioneer Company;
19. Pioneer Company documents, proving the overall status of the company;
20. A copy of Song Xingting's ID card, proving his identity and the authenticity of his signature on the full-power authorization letter for vessel operation;
21. A letter of authorization, proving that Pioneer Company authorized Tianhai Company to claim externally for losses incurred due to the detention of the M/V "SALVIA II" in Indonesia (including vessel depreciation);
22. A copy of Zhou Xikang's ID card and a certificate from his current employer, the Chinese Consulate General in San Francisco;
23. 11 fresh water expense receipts for the M/V "SALVIA II", 33 crew medical expense receipts, and 5 crew labor service fee receipts;
24. Tianhai Company's calculation of fuel costs during the detention period of the M/V "SALVIA II";
25. Two engineering settlement statements and repair invoices for emergency repairs of the M/V "SALVIA II" at the outer anchorage of Shekou issued by Shenzhen Shunzhu Port Machinery Equipment Co., Ltd., one repair invoice for the M/V "SALVIA II" issued by Shenzhen Jiahang Ship and Offshore Engineering Equipment Co., Ltd., and one repair settlement statement and repair invoice for the M/V "SALVIA II" issued by the Dagang Repair Station of Jiangyin Chengxi Shipyard;
26. Tianhai Company's accounting vouchers for calculating depreciation of the M/V "SALVIA II";
27. An audit report issued by Tianjin Jinlian Accounting Firm on November 22, 2001, after reviewing the amount of Tianhai Company's losses;
28. Documents evidencing Tianhai Company's payment for the purchase of the M/V "SALVIA II".

**Defendant's Defense:** The M/V "SALVIA II" was chartered to carry cargo to Indonesia through a series of charter parties, and there was no contractual relationship between the plaintiff and the defendant. The plaintiff is not the owner of the vessel and thus has no interest in the vessel. Any expenses incurred by the plaintiff as an operator during the period of vessel detention can only be claimed from the vessel owner under the operation contract, and the plaintiff is not entitled to claim directly from the defendant. Moreover, the defendant never instructed the vessel to carry cargo without lawful documentation, and therefore is not obligated to provide lawful documentation for the cargo on board. The master agreed to load the cargo before the joint inspection was completed, which violated local law and led to the seizure. The defendant had no fault in this matter. Therefore, the plaintiff's claim has no connection with the defendant and should be dismissed.

The defendant, Yuedong Company, submitted the following evidence:

1. The voyage charter party for the M/V "SALVIA II" dated April 25, 2000, signed between Yuedong Company and Ruihang Company;
2. Telegrams sent by the master of the M/V "SALVIA II" on May 4, May 5, May 9, May 18, May 20, and June 15, 2000, reporting the vessel's movements and the progress of the seizure. The telegram of May 9 stated that the joint inspection had been completed;
3. The judgment of the Sorong District Court in Indonesia, to prove that the vessel was seized because the master failed to complete the vessel's entry formalities. The master was at fault, and Tianhai Company should bear the responsibility itself;
4. China Shipowners' Mutual Assurance Association Bulletin No. 007 [2000], to prove that the seizure was caused by the master of the M/V "SALVIA II" trusting the shipper too readily;
5. A letter dated June 16, 2000, from Tianhai Company, proving the course of events;
6. A letter dated October 8, 2000, from Tianhai Company to Ruihang Company, to prove that Tianhai Company unilaterally terminated the time charter party with Ruihang Company;
7. The vessel registration certificate and Lloyd's register data for the M/V "SALVIA II", to prove that the owner of the M/V "SALVIA II" is not Tianhai Company.

During the court investigation, the parties examined each other's evidence. After examination and verification, the Tianjin Maritime Court found the following facts:

On March 1, 1995, Pioneer Company, the owner of the M/V "SALVIA II", issued a "Full-Power Authorization for Vessel Operation", entrusting the plaintiff, Tianhai Company, with full authority to operate and manage the vessel. All debts and credits arising from the operation and management of the vessel, Tianhai Company was entitled to exercise rights and assume liabilities in its own name against third parties.

On August 1, 1999, the plaintiff, Tianhai Company, signed a "Crew Employment Agreement" with the Shipping Department of China Guangdong International Cooperation (Group) Corporation, employing Master Lin Huahui and 20 crew members from that department to work on the M/V "SALVIA II". Crew wages and food allowances were handled on a lump-sum basis, with a monthly contract amount of USD 15,833.33. The agreement was valid until July 31, 2001.

On April 17, 2000, the plaintiff, Tianhai Company, signed a "Time Charter Party" with Ruihang Company on behalf of the vessel owner, Pioneer Company. It was agreed that Ruihang Company would charter the M/V "SALVIA II" to carry lawful cargo, with a charter period of 3 months plus 3 months at the charterer's option, and a hire rate of USD 2,250 per day.

On April 25, 2000, Ruihang Company, as the disponent owner, signed a "Voyage Charter Party" with the defendant, Yuedong Company. It was agreed that Yuedong Company, as the charterer, would charter the M/V "SALVIA II" to proceed to a safe anchorage in Sorong, Indonesia, to load 5,000 cubic meters of logs, and then to proceed to a safe anchorage in Kuantan, Malaysia, to discharge. The laycan for loading was May 7 to May 11, 2000. Laytime was to commence after the cargo documents were ready at the loading port. If loading could not be completed on time due to cargo or cargo document reasons, the charterer was to pay demurrage. The charterer was to provide agents at the loading and discharging ports and pay port charges, including agency fees, provide stevedores and pay their costs, and tally fees. Before the vessel's arrival at the loading and discharging ports, the charterer was to obtain the cargo export license and pay the costs incurred thereby. All costs relating to the cargo were to be paid by the charterer. On April 29, Ruihang Company issued a voyage order to the M/V "SALVIA II". On May 1, Yuedong Company wrote to Ruihang Company stating that the details of the agent at the loading port would be provided the next day. On May 2, Yuedong Company again wrote to Ruihang Company stating that because the shipper had not yet returned to Indonesia, only a general agent notification could be provided.

On May 5, 2000, the M/V "SALVIA II", in accordance with Ruihang Company's voyage order, arrived at the outer anchorage of Sorong, Indonesia, and anchored, awaiting joint inspection. On May 9, the defendant, Yuedong Company, wrote to Ruihang Company stating that the shipper had prepared two barges carrying a total of 2,000 cubic meters of logs, could load that evening, and would make every effort to complete loading as quickly as possible. On the same day, stevedores boarded the vessel, the agent boarded, and the joint inspection was completed. On May 10, the barges came alongside, and at 13:00 local time, stevedores began loading.

On May 16, officials from Indonesian customs and immigration boarded the M/V "SALVIA II" for inspection.

On May 18, Ruihang Company wrote to the defendant, Yuedong Company, stating: "The M/V 'SALVIA II' has completed loading, but the agent has still not gone to the Indonesian immigration authorities and local customs to handle the vessel's departure formalities. The vessel will be towed into the port. If any loss arises, Yuedong Company, as the charterer, shall bear full responsibility." On the same day, Yuedong Company wrote to Ruihang Company stating: "We have contacted the shipper and requested that the agent depart immediately and do everything possible to resolve this matter."

On May 19, Indonesian customs issued an order to the M/V "SALVIA II": "Because the shipper of the cargo you are carrying has not completed customs clearance procedures or obtained a log export certificate, although the master is unaware of how the shipper arranged this, customs nevertheless orders you to sail to Sorong port to complete customs clearance and other formalities." On the same day, Indonesian customs issued another order to the M/V "SALVIA II": "Based on the cargo problem on your vessel, you are not permitted to sail without approval from Indonesian customs." Also on the same day, the M/V "SALVIA II" telegraphed the plaintiff, Tianhai Company, stating: "Because the shipper's documentation is incomplete, after loading the cargo, Indonesian customs and immigration have refused permission to sail." On the same day, the M/V "SALVIA II" telexed Ruihang Company stating: "Because the shipper has not paid customs duties, and the shipper has no commercial license or export license for the trade and export of this type of log, the vessel cannot sail until these two issues are resolved."

On May 22, the M/V "SALVIA II" telegraphed the plaintiff, Tianhai Company, stating: "The logs loaded on this vessel have been formally detained by Indonesian customs."

On May 25, the M/V "SALVIA II" telexed Ruihang Company stating: "The questions from Indonesian customs must be answered, otherwise the vessel will be detained. The agent and shipper may escape. If Indonesian customs cannot catch them, they may turn their attention to the vessel." On the same day, the M/V "SALVIA II" telegraphed the plaintiff, Tianhai Company, stating: "Because the shipper has been absent for a long time and has not completed procedures with Indonesian customs, customs requires answers to: (1) the name of the shipper; (2) the name of the consignee and the details of the sales contract. Please order Ruihang Company to provide this information before noon to avoid vessel seizure. Urgent."

The defendant, Yuedong Company, never provided any information about the agent or the shipper. On May 27, the M/V "SALVIA II" telexed Ruihang Company stating: "This incident has been referred by Indonesian customs to the local court."

On May 31, the M/V "SALVIA II" telexed Ruihang Company stating: "Indonesian customs have been unable to catch the shipper and the agent, so they suspect the vessel of smuggling. They have informed the vessel to engage a lawyer within one week before the local court begins the trial. If the shipper and agent do not contact Indonesian customs again, the cargo will be confiscated, and the vessel and the master will bear responsibility."

On June 15, the M/V "SALVIA II" telegraphed the plaintiff, Tianhai Company, stating: "Twenty people from Indonesian customs and related departments have boarded the vessel and are taking the master ashore. Please contact the Chinese Embassy in Indonesia and all other parties immediately to arrange a lawyer and interpreter for the vessel. Urgent."

On June 26, July 6, and July 31, the Chinese Embassy in Indonesia sent notes verbales to the Indonesian Ministry of Foreign Affairs, the Attorney General's Office, and the Supreme Prosecutor's Office, respectively, stating the Chinese government's position: the M/V "SALVIA II," its vessel, and its crew were not involved in smuggling, and requesting the withdrawal of charges against the master and the early release of the vessel and personnel.

On September 23, the Sorong District Court in Indonesia found Lin Huahui, master of the M/V "SALVIA II," guilty of "attempting to export export goods from Indonesian customs territory without lawful documentation," in violation of local law, and sentenced him to seven months' imprisonment and a fine of 30 million Indonesian rupiah or six months' imprisonment, with the sentence suspended for one year.

On October 28, the M/V "SALVIA II" departed from Sorong, Indonesia.

The plaintiff, Tianhai Company, sent staff members Cao Wei and Zhang Jiapei to Indonesia to resolve the detention of the M/V "SALVIA II." Zhou Xikang, a secretary from the Chinese Embassy in Indonesia, and Ye Sunfa, an Indonesian Chinese, participated in resolving the matter in Sorong.

On October 20, 2001, Pioneer Company issued another letter of authorization, confirming that the plaintiff, Tianhai Company, as the operator of the M/V "SALVIA II," was entitled to and liable for all debts and credits related to the vessel, and specifically authorized Tianhai Company to claim externally for all expenses or losses incurred due to the detention of the M/V "SALVIA II" in Indonesia, including repair costs and depreciation.

The losses incurred by the plaintiff, Tianhai Company, for which formal receipts exist include: (1) Payments demanded by Indonesian immigration, prosecutor's office, and quarantine authorities from Master Lin Huahui, totaling USD 11,413.69. (2) Lawyer fees incurred for Master Lin Huahui, totaling USD 11,414.15. (3) Fees for protection agents engaged by Tianhai Company, totaling USD 2,500. (4) Translation fees of USD 202.50. (5) Travel expenses incurred by Tianhai Company in sending staff Cao Wei, Zhang Jiapei, and others to Shantou and Sorong, Indonesia, to handle the matter, totaling USD 7,013 and RMB 35,951. (6) Communication costs in Indonesia and crew land transportation costs, totaling USD 3,829.18. (7) Crew medical expenses of USD 590.22. (8) Crew labor service fees of RMB 38,150. (9) Payment for vessel fuel of USD 8,320. (10) Fresh water costs of USD 3,888.87. All of the above amounts have been verified by Tianjin Jinlian Limited Liability Accounting Firm upon review.

In the time charter party between the plaintiff, Tianhai Company, and Ruihang Company, the charter period was agreed as 3 months plus 3 months at the charterer's option. Calculated from the date of the contract, April 17, 2000, the first 3-month period expired on July 17, 2000. The charterer, Ruihang Company, did not indicate its intention to continue with the subsequent 3-month period, and had already stopped paying hire before the expiry of the first 3-month period. This should be regarded as its choice not to extend the charter. During the 80-day period from July 18, 2000, to October 28, 2000, the loss of crew hire paid by Tianhai Company was USD 42,222.21, and the vessel depreciation loss for the M/V "SALVIA II" was RMB 509,816.92 (based on the annual depreciation of RMB 3,367,560 recorded in the accounting vouchers, the 80-day depreciation should be RMB 738,095.34).

On October 27, 2000, the M/V "SALVIA II" underwent emergency repairs at the outer anchorage of Shekou, Shenzhen, including diving to remove marine growth and other restorative repairs. The plaintiff, Tianhai Company, paid repair costs of RMB 219,236. From December 20, 2000, to January 1, 2001, the M/V "SALVIA II" underwent restorative repairs to the deck section at the Dagang Repair Station of Jiangyin Chengxi Shipyard, and Tianhai Company paid repair costs of RMB 168,066.

The above facts are confirmed by evidence submitted and certified by both parties.

**Opinion of the Tianjin Maritime Court:**

The plaintiff, Tianhai Company, as the operator of the M/V "SALVIA II", operated and managed the vessel pursuant to the authorization of the vessel owner, Pioneer Company, assumed the debts and credits related to the vessel, and lawfully obtained the rights to possess, use, and derive profit from the vessel. Tianhai Company's execution of the "Crew Employment Agreement" with the Shipping Department of China Guangdong International Cooperation (Group) Corporation and the "Time Charter Party" with Ruihang Company constituted an exercise of its operational rights over the M/V "SALVIA II". After the vessel was detained in Indonesia, Tianhai Company, as the shipowner, actively participated in activities to resolve the incident, sought assistance from the Chinese Embassy in Indonesia, paid relevant fees to the Indonesian authorities, and ultimately obtained the vessel's release. Tianhai Company is entitled to claim compensation for the resulting losses from the liable party. Regarding the vessel depreciation loss during this period, because Pioneer Company specifically authorized it, Tianhai Company is entitled to claim it as well. The action brought by Tianhai Company in this case is an infringement action; therefore, it may only claim compensation for losses incurred outside the contract. The loss of hire during the 3-month contract period between Tianhai Company and Ruihang Company shall be resolved separately by Tianhai Company and Ruihang Company in another proceeding.

The defendant Yuedong Company obtained the right to use the vessel "SALVIA Ⅱ" from Ruihang Company by entering into a "Voyage Charter Party" for transporting Indonesian logs. Article 7 of the Contract Law of the People's Republic of China provides: "The parties to a contract shall conclude and perform the contract in accordance with the laws and administrative regulations." The contract between Yuedong Company and Ruihang Company stipulated that Yuedong Company was responsible for handling documents such as the export license for the goods and providing loading and discharging port agents, and paying the relevant fees. Accordingly, Yuedong Company was obligated under both contract and law to ensure transaction security. Yuedong Company had the responsibility to properly handle customs declarations and export licenses for the relevant goods and to provide port agents capable of fulfilling their duties.

In this case, before and during loading, the shipper failed to complete the customs declaration and export license procedures for the logs in accordance with the laws of the country where the loading occurred, and the loading port agent also failed to perform its duties. When the Indonesian customs authorities required the supplementary procedures to be completed, the shipper and the loading port agent abandoned the cargo on board and fled. At this point, the defendant Yuedong Company should have actively engaged with the Indonesian customs authorities, come forward to explain the situation, and assume responsibility, but it failed to perform this obligation actively and appropriately. Consequently, the vessel and its master were detained on suspicion of involvement in smuggling, and the master was sentenced by an Indonesian court, thereby infringing upon the rights of possession, use, benefit, and disposal enjoyed by the plaintiff Tianhai Company, as the vessel operator, and Pioneer Company, as the vessel owner. Yuedong Company should bear liability.

The Sorong District Court of Indonesia sentenced Lin Huahui, the master of the "SALVIA Ⅱ," on the grounds that he "attempted to export products from Indonesian customs territory without the protection of lawful documents." The facts demonstrate that Lin Huahui did not "attempt to export products from Indonesian customs territory"; the reason for the "absence of lawful document protection" was that after the shipper and loading port agent, who were aware of the true condition of the cargo, fled, the defendant Yuedong Company failed to come forward to explain the situation and assume responsibility. The Chinese Embassy in Indonesia had already issued a statement on behalf of the Chinese government declaring that the vessel "SALVIA Ⅱ" and its crew were not involved in smuggling. Moreover, the China Shipowners' Mutual Assurance Association had never participated in the resolution of this incident, and its notification is inconsistent with the facts of this case. Therefore, Yuedong Company's reliance on the judgment of the Sorong District Court of Indonesia and the notification of the China Shipowners' Mutual Assurance Association as evidence of its non-liability is unfounded.

Article 106, paragraph 2, of the General Principles of the Civil Law of the People's Republic of China provides: "A citizen or legal person who, through fault, infringes upon state or collective property or infringes upon the property or person of others shall bear civil liability." During the period the "SALVIA Ⅱ" was detained in Indonesia, the plaintiff Tianhai Company incurred expenses including fines, legal fees, translation fees, travel expenses paid to secure the release of the vessel and its master, as well as fees for protection agents, crew labor costs, transportation and communication fees, medical expenses, freshwater costs, fuel costs, crew hire, and vessel repair costs incurred to protect the vessel's safety and safeguard the crew's rights and interests. The defendant Yuedong Company shall compensate all of these costs. As for the vessel depreciation losses incurred during the detention period, since the vessel owner authorized Tianhai Company to claim them jointly, Yuedong Company shall also compensate for these losses. Tianhai Company voluntarily chose to claim an amount less than that recorded in its accounting vouchers, which is a civil act of disposing of its own property rights. This act does not harm the interests of Yuedong Company and should be recognized. Accordingly, on November 30, 2001, Tianjin Maritime Court rendered the following judgment:

1. The defendant Yuedong Company shall compensate the plaintiff Tianhai Company for losses including fines, etc., amounting to RMB 1,727,046.80, and shall pay interest thereon at the bank deposit rate for the corresponding period, calculated from October 29, 2000, until the date of payment determined by the judgment.

The above amounts shall be paid in full within 15 days from the date this judgment takes effect. In case of delay in payment, execution shall be carried out in accordance with Article 232 of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China.

2. The other claims of the plaintiff Tianhai Company are dismissed.

The case acceptance fee of RMB 23,506, the preservation application fee of RMB 13,020, and the preservation execution fee of RMB 3,000, totaling RMB 39,526, shall be borne by the defendant Yuedong Company.

After the judgment was pronounced, Yuedong Company, dissatisfied, filed an appeal with the Tianjin High People's Court, seeking to overturn the original judgment and dismiss the complaint or claims of the appellee Tianhai Company. The grounds were: (1) The appellant obtained the use of the vessel "SALVIA Ⅱ" by entering into a contract with Ruihang Company. Since Ruihang Company did not participate in the lawsuit, Tianhai Company, relying solely on the letter of authorization from Pioneer Company to initiate the action, is not a proper subject of the litigation. (2) The original judgment was erroneous in its findings of fact. Since Ruihang Company did not participate in the lawsuit and Tianhai Company did not produce the original document, a copy cannot be used as evidence. The original judgment's finding, based on a copy, that Tianhai Company, on behalf of Pioneer Company, entered into a "Time Charter Party" with Ruihang Company was erroneous. Master Lin Huahui is an interested party in this case, and using his testimony as the basis for factual findings was erroneous. The 67 telegrams were all copies and, except for those confirmed by the appellant, cannot be used as evidence. The judgment of the Sorong District Court of Indonesia found that the master's failure to complete the relevant procedures led to the vessel's detention, and the facts so found by the court should, of course, constitute evidence. The agent involved in this case should be defined as an agent appointed by the shipowner, not a charterer's agent. The original judgment's resolution of the liability issues arising from the agent's dereliction of duty based on the charterer's agent concept was a misinterpretation of Clause 14 of the Voyage Charter Party. (3) The appellant engaged in no tortious conduct in this case and should bear no liability. (4) The appellant's chartering of the "SALVIA Ⅱ" only established a contractual relationship with the lessor, Ruihang Company. Having paid the hire as charterer, all crew expenses, vessel depreciation, and repair costs should be borne by the lessor Ruihang Company. The original judgment's order for the appellant to compensate for these expenses was erroneous.

The appellee Tianhai Company argued in its defense that the original judgment was factually clear and legally correct, and that the appeal should be dismissed and the original judgment affirmed. The reasons were: (1) Tianhai Company, having been duly authorized by the owner of "SALVIA Ⅱ," Pioneer Company, has the right to dispose of the vessel's rights and is a proper subject of the litigation. (2) The original judgment's findings of fact were clear and correct. Tianhai Company had produced the original of the "Time Charter Party," and the court had verified the copy against the original. The Indonesian court, in safeguarding its own national interests and being unable to locate the shipper and agent, attributed the fault to the master, which did not accurately reflect the objective facts. The court of first instance was correct in not admitting that judgment as evidence, as this upheld both the dignity of Chinese law and the truth of the matter. The court of first instance was also correct in determining, based on Clause 14 of the Voyage Charter Party, that the loading port agent in this case was a charterer's agent, thereby imposing on the charterer liability for losses arising from the agent's failure to perform its duties. (3) Although the appellant Yuedong Company chartered the "SALVIA Ⅱ" after entering into the contract, its failure to perform its obligations not only breached the contract but also directly infringed the rights of the appellee. The appellant's tortious conduct was evident, and the court of first instance properly ordered it only to compensate for losses incurred outside the contract period.

During the second instance, the parties disputed the proper translation and interpretation of the original text of Clause 14 of the Voyage Charter Party: "Owns appoint chtrs Nomi agt Bends wz chtrs paying portcharge (INCL agency fee) before vsl arrival at bends."

The appellant Yuedong Company had this clause translated by the Guangdong Notary Public Office. The translation was: "The shipowner appoints the agent nominated by the charterer at both loading and discharging ports. Port charges (including agency fees) are to be paid by the charterer before the vessel's arrival at the ports."

The appellee Tianhai Company had it translated by the Tianjin Foreign Cultural Development Managerial Department. The translation was: "The lessor appoints, at both loading and discharging ports, the agent designated by the charterer. Port charges (including agency fees) are to be paid by the charterer before the vessel's arrival at the ports."

The appellee Tianhai Company applied for a new translation. In accordance with the principle of Article 28 of the Supreme People's Court's Several Provisions on Evidence in Civil Proceedings, which states that "where a party has independently commissioned a relevant department to make an expert conclusion, and the other party has sufficient evidence to rebut it and applies for a new appraisal, the people's court shall permit it," the court facilitated consultations between the parties regarding the appointment of a translator. Based on the consensus reached by the parties, Professor Yin Dongnian of Shanghai Maritime University was commissioned to translate and interpret Clause 14 of the Voyage Charter Party.

Professor Yin Dongnian's translation was: "The shipowner designates the agent designated by the charterer as the agent at both loading and discharging ports. Port charges (including agency fees) are to be paid by the charterer before the vessel's arrival at the ports." Professor Yin Dongnian further explained: (1) "Owns" is usually translated as "shipowner." Since the "Fixture Note" involved in this case is a confirmation under a voyage charter, based on the principle of privity of contract, this confirmation only applies to the two main parties to the voyage charter, namely Ruihang Company and Yuedong Company. The shipowner referred to in this clause is the former, commonly known in shipping practice as the "disponent owner." The shipowner referred to here is not the actual shipowner (commonly known as the "head owner") or vessel operator. (2) "Appoint" and "Nomi," according to shipping industry practice, are best translated as "designate." Translating them as "appoint" or "nominate" would be inappropriate in this context.

The appellant Yuedong Company argued that Professor Yin Dongnian's translation of "Nomi" as "designate" was inappropriate. The appellee Tianhai Company had no objection to Professor Yin Dongnian's translation and interpretation.

The Tianjin High People's Court held as follows:

The translation and interpretation commissioned by the court, after consultation and with the unanimous agreement of the parties, from a distinguished expert regarding Clause 14 of the Voyage Charter Party is authoritative and should be confirmed.

The appellee Tianhai Company, having obtained the right to operate the "SALVIA Ⅱ" by virtue of authorization, had the right to enter into a contract with Ruihang Company for the charter of the vessel. The appellant Yuedong Company, by entering into the Voyage Charter Party, obtained the right to use the "SALVIA Ⅱ" for this particular voyage from Ruihang Company to transport Indonesian logs, thereby assuming the obligation to ensure that the cargo being transported did not affect the vessel's safety. Because Yuedong Company failed to fulfill its due obligations, causing harm to Tianhai Company's interests, Tianhai Company is entitled to assert its rights against Yuedong Company. Yuedong Company's argument on appeal that Tianhai Company lacks standing to sue is unfounded.

The judgment of the Sorong District Court of Indonesia against Master Lin Huahui was rendered under a different sovereign jurisdiction. The facts found in that judgment have not been verified by the judicial authorities of China and cannot be admitted as evidence in this case. The appellant Yuedong Company's request to rely on this judgment as evidence of the facts in this case is unfounded.

Clause 14 of the Voyage Charter Party between the appellant Yuedong Company and Ruihang Company provides that the shipowner designates the agent designated by the charterer as the agent at both loading and discharging ports. Yuedong Company, as the charterer, was responsible for designating the loading and discharging port agents to the shipowner and did, in fact, designate the loading port agent for the shipowner. However, the shipper, whom Yuedong Company had arranged, failed to obtain the necessary legal documents for the cargo, and the loading port agent designated by Yuedong Company was also negligent in performing its duties. When the customs authorities required the supplementary procedures, the shipper and the loading port agent abandoned the vessel and fled. After these problems arose, Yuedong Company failed to actively engage with the Indonesian authorities to explain the situation and assume responsibility, thereby causing losses to the appellee Tianhai Company. It is therefore liable for compensation.

The appellant Yuedong Company shall compensate the appellee Tianhai Company for the fines, legal fees, translation fees, and travel expenses paid to secure the release of the vessel and its master, as well as the protection agent fees, crew labor costs, transportation and communication fees, medical expenses, freshwater costs, fuel costs, and crew hire paid for the safety of the vessel and crew after the expiration of the three-month charter period. Vessel depreciation losses arose due to Yuedong Company's failure to fulfill its obligations, resulting in the prolonged detention of the vessel after the three-month charter period. The prolonged detention in a hot and humid region caused significant damage to the vessel's bottom, cargo holds, and other parts, necessitating restorative repairs, giving rise to repair costs. Since the vessel owner Pioneer Company authorized Tianhai Company to claim these losses, Yuedong Company shall also compensate for them. The amount of losses claimed by Tianhai Company is supported by documentary evidence. The original judgment had already deducted the portion that should be borne by Ruihang Company within the contract period. Yuedong Company argues on appeal that the losses found by the original judgment should be borne by Ruihang Company, but it fails to provide evidence for this assertion. Therefore, this ground of appeal is also unfounded.

In summary, the original judgment has clear factual findings, correct application of law, and a proper decision, and should be affirmed. The appellant Yuedong Company's grounds of appeal lack factual and legal basis and are not supported. Accordingly, the Tianjin High People's Court, pursuant to Article 153, paragraph 1, item (1) of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, rendered the following judgment on August 13, 2002:

The appeal is dismissed, and the original judgment is affirmed.

The second-instance case acceptance fee of RMB 23,506 shall be borne by the appellant Yuedong Company.

After the final judgment was served, both parties voluntarily reached a settlement agreement, and the obligations determined in the judgment have been performed.
